CHAPTER III — CONTROLS & INTERFACE
3.1 RTS Controls
Obsidian Protocol uses a modern RTS control scheme designed for speed, clarity, and intent‑based commanding. The interface minimizes micromanagement and maximizes strategic expression.

Core Navigation
Left‑Click — Select unit, building, or UI element

Right‑Click — Issue contextual intent (move, attack, interact)

Middle‑Mouse / Scroll — Zoom in/out (strategic zoom)

WASD / Edge Pan — Move camera

Q/E — Rotate camera

Shift — Queue intents or multi‑select

Ctrl — Add/remove units from selection

Selection Tools
Click‑Drag Box — Select multiple units

Double‑Click — Select all units of the same type on screen

Ctrl+Number — Assign control group

Number Key — Recall control group

Strategic Zoom
The camera transitions seamlessly from:

Macro view (entire battlefield)

Operational view (frontlines, squads, command units)

Micro view (individual units, breach teams, drones)

Zoom level affects:

Visibility cones

Sensor overlays

Command radius visualization

3.2 Command Wheel
The Command Wheel is the core interface for intent‑based commanding. It replaces traditional micromanaged orders with high‑level strategic directives.

Wheel Structure
When a unit or squad is selected, pressing R (default) opens the wheel:

Primary Intents
Assault — breach, flank, suppress

Recon — scout, shadow, track

Hold — defend, anchor, intercept

Support — repair, escort, reinforce

Strike — precision attack, bombard, disrupt

Retreat — fall back, regroup, reposition

Intent Modifiers
Holding Shift while selecting an intent adds modifiers:

Aggressive — prioritize damage

Cautious — prioritize survival

Silent — minimize detection

Rapid — maximize speed

Stealth — avoid visibility cones

Intent Preview
Before confirming, the wheel shows:

Predicted squad path

Expected breach point

Suppression zones

Visibility cones

Estimated risk level

This preview helps players make informed decisions without micromanagement.

3.3 Tactical Overlays
Tactical overlays visualize battlefield information that autonomous squads use internally.

Overlay Types
Visibility Cones — directional sightlines

Suppression Zones — areas of heavy fire

Elevation Map — height advantages and disadvantages

Cover Indicators — destructible and non‑destructible cover

Sensor Fields — LIDAR, thermal, night vision

Command Radius — influence area of command units

Logistics Lines — supply routes, repair drone paths

Hazard Zones — fire, radiation, EMP, flooding

Overlay Controls
Tab — toggle overlays

Shift+Tab — cycle overlay types

Alt — hold to show all overlays temporarily

Overlays are designed to be readable without cluttering the battlefield.

3.4 VR Controls
VR modes use a hybrid control scheme that blends cockpit simulation with tactical operator functionality.

Movement & Interaction
Head Movement — look around cockpit or drone view

VR Controllers — interact with cockpit panels, switches, and overlays

Trigger — fire weapons or activate tools

Grip — stabilize aim or interact with physical controls

Thumbstick — maneuver drones or navigate operator interface

VR Comfort Options
Snap turning

Smooth turning

Cockpit stabilization

Reduced shake mode

High‑fidelity mode

3.5 VR Cockpit HUD
The VR HUD is a fully diegetic cockpit interface for dogfights and air‑unit piloting.

HUD Elements
Radar Sphere — 360° situational awareness

Thermal Overlay — heat signatures

LIDAR Grid — structural outlines

Missile Lock Indicator — tone + visual cue

Damage Panel — hull, engine, avionics, weapons

Afterburner Meter — energy management

Altitude Bands — engagement layers

Threat Arcs — incoming fire direction

Cockpit Interactions
Players can:

Flip switches

Adjust sensors

Arm weapons

Deploy countermeasures

Switch between flight modes

The cockpit is designed to feel tactile and immersive.

3.6 Drone‑Jumping Interface
Drone‑jumping allows players to inhabit any drone or sensor unit in real time.

Activation
Select drone

Press F (default)

Instantly transition into VR or first‑person view

Drone Capabilities
Manual recon

Thermal sweep

LIDAR scan

Night vision

Hazard marking

Laser designation

Manual strike targeting

Operator Tools
Inside drone view, players can:

Tag enemies

Mark breach points

Highlight suppression zones

Identify stealth units

Relay sensor data to squads

Drone‑jumping is a core part of VR Operator Mode and integrates directly with RTS intent‑based commanding.
